/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  cstr08_furusawa_bistable  --  an ADIABATIC CSTR on a measured exothermic
  reaction, scanned for steady states.  It finds THREE.

  THE SOURCE.  Furusawa, Nishimura & Miyauchi, "Experimental study of a
  bistable continuous stirred-tank reactor", J. Chem. Eng. Japan 2 (1969)
  95-100, doi:10.1252/jcej.2.95 (open on J-STAGE).  They ran propylene-oxide
  hydration, acid-catalysed, in an insulated Dewar CSTR, chosen "because of
  its high heat of reaction, its high activation energy and its significant
  reaction rate at room temperature", and ran three conditions (Table 1):

      condition   tau [s]   T0 [K]    what they measured
      1            980      274.3     one steady state, LOW conversion
      2           1330      274.6     two stable states (three crossings)
      3           1550      287.1     one steady state, HIGH conversion

  WHAT THIS CASE TAKES FROM THE PAPER, and nothing else is tuned:
    * the kinetics, first order in propylene oxide, their Eq. (11):
      k = 4.71e9 exp(-18.0 kcal/mol / RT) 1/s   (constant/reactions);
    * the feed: an equal-volume propylene-oxide + methanol solution mixed
      with acidified water to 2.15 mol/L of propylene oxide.  Per litre of
      feed, with the record volumes added (the paper's own assumption 2,
      no volume change): 2.15 mol oxide, 3.72 mol methanol, 38.58 mol water
      -- the molar flows in 0/feed are that composition at 0.01 kmol/h of
      oxide;
    * the inlet temperature: the controlled 274.3 K PLUS the 10 K the heat
      of DILUTION adds on mixing, as the paper itself does ("the inlet
      temperature of the reactor could then be regarded as being 10.0 deg.
      higher than the controlled value"): 284.3 K.  An ideal liquid has no
      heat of mixing, so the paper's correction is the honest one here;
    * the residence time of CONDITION 1, 980 s: V_R below is the feed's
      volumetric flow (1.292e-6 m3/s on the record volumes) times 980 s;
    * adiabatic: the measured heat loss was under 1 % of what the effluent
      carries away, and is left out.

  WHAT THE ENGINE ADDS, and where it parts from the paper.  The heat of
  reaction is not declared: it is priced from the formation enthalpies of
  the four records on an IDEAL liquid, the glycol on its measured liquid
  reference (-501.0 kJ/mol, Knauth & Sabbah).  At complete conversion that gives
  an adiabatic rise of 57.8 K (the scan ceiling, 342.06 K, minus 284.3 K).  The
  paper MEASURED 45.5 K in a batch Dewar and 48.6 K in the running reactor,
  and used 46.8 K: the engine releases about 24 % more heat than the real
  mixture did.  The gap is not chased here; an ideal liquid carries no heats
  of solution of the oxide and the glycol in water, which is where a
  calorimeter and a table of pure-liquid formation enthalpies part.

  The consequence is the lesson.  The three-crossing window is narrow (the
  paper calls its own condition-2 phase plane "structurally unstable"), and
  24 % more heat moves it to SHORTER residence times.  So the engine finds
  three crossings at condition 1, where the experiment saw one cold state:

      292.43 K   extinguished, X = 0.14   stable
      302.61 K   middle                   unstable (the slope test fails)
      336.55 K   ignited,      X = 0.90   stable -- reported (T_guess 340)

  (the liquid density of the property kernel since the C378 pilot: the three
  were 292.22, 303.03 and 336.48 K on the record volumes at 25 C), and at
  condition 2 (tau 1330 s, 284.6 K) it finds only the ignited state,
  338.8 K, where the experiment saw two; at condition 3 (tau 1550 s, 297.1 K)
  one ignited state, 353.9 K, as measured (these two on the record volumes
  at 25 C, before the pilot; not recomputed).  The paper's own model, with its
  measured 46.8 K, puts three crossings at condition 2 (295.8, 302.4 and
  320.9 K, by hand from Eq. (11)).  Read the case as the van Heerden
  construction on a real, measured rate law -- not as a reproduction of the
  measured states.

      phi(T) = H_out(T) - H_in - Q_ext(T) = 0

  Eliminate the extent and what remains is ONE equation in the temperature,
  the textbook "heat generated equals heat removed".  The generation curve
  G(T) is a sigmoid (the rate saturates once the reactant is gone); the
  removal line R(T) = F cp (T - T_in) is straight (adiabatic: the only sink
  is the sensible heat that leaves with the product).
  A straight line can cut a sigmoid THREE times:
  the extinguished state, an unstable middle state, and the ignited state.
  Choupo scans, finds them all, prints them, and reports the one nearest
  `T_guess`.  Change T_guess and the reactor moves to another branch -- the
  same equipment, the same feed, a different answer.  That is not a
  numerical defect; it is the physics, and it is why real reactors need a
  start-up procedure.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        reactor;
        type        cstr;
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( reactorOut );
        operation
        {
            V_R          0.00126615;    // m^3 = 1.29199e-6 m3/s x 980 s (condition 1)
            thermalMode  adiabatic;     // the removal line is pure sensible heat
            T_guess      340;            // start point of the Newton: lands on the
                                         //   IGNITED state; 290 lands on the cold one
        }
        reactions   ( hydration_PO );
    }
);
